speechtext:
I do not apologize. An undeserved compliment, Mr. Speaker. You have shown such a judicial mind that I judged you to be a lawyer. But from your experience, it will occur to you as one of the most outrageous things that could possibly be suggested that the counsel for the defence should confer with the witnesses for the prosecution. It is only a little thing, but it shows exactly how this matter is going. We have had, day after day, the harmless Mr. Chrysler going over a lot of stuff and bringing in letters which were intended to damage Mr. Lumsden; though my opinion is that in these matters the opinion of counsel is of no weight whatever. Mr, Chrysler brings in these letters and a lot of evidence and sits down. Mr. Smith then goes on from day to day. When Mr. Smith omits anything, Mr. Chrysler suggests the omission, and cures it; if he does not, my hon. friend from Pictou comes to the rescue; and so it goes on from day to day, and from week to week, at- an expense of,
I suppose, |1,000 a day to the country. But that is a small matter compared to the enormous expenditure involved. For my part, when I retired, I thought I was serving the public best by doing so in protest against the attitude assumed by the government with their long array of counsel, fortified by the Minister of Justice. In reference to Mr. Wallace Nesbitt, let me say that when his opinion came to Ottawa two years ago, from whom do you think it came? It came from the firm of Ayles-worth and Company, the firm headed by the Minister of Justice; and to-day a member of that same firm is fighting for the position taken' by the commission, which is fighting for the postion taken by the government; and they are all Chrysler, -Smith, Atkinson in the office, the lawyer at the head of the committee, the government counsel, the able, sapient and potent Solicitor General, overworked as he is, in sending out this false literature to the country-all fighting against the people and the people's interest. So I thought I would select my field of battle outside of that committee;

where I could do better service for the public, and not remain there as a buffer bef tween the people and those who deserve the people's condemnation. I retired, and I am willing to abide by the issue. May I, in closing, appeal to the Prime Minister? It is said that we are in contempt. We are not willingly in contempt. We can, of course, be put in the tower for our wrongdoing; I recognize that, and for the interest of the country I am willing to sacrifice myself even to that extent. But I beseech the Prime Minister, as my parting word, to send me to the solidly-built tower of this building and not to the Laurier tower, so liable at any time to fall again.
